1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
I run PitBot, for sim-racing leagues. When a driver protests a move, a volunteer steward digs through the race footage to rule on it.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
I'm Brock. I built faultline: give it written rules and footage, and it rules on every rule, with the evidence.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The same judge reads a warehouse, where forklifts hurt people and nobody watches every camera.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Its rulings serve two people: the safety lead, who gets a short list to act on, and the insurer, who gets evidence instead of a form.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The site writes its rules in plain English. Meta's open-weight Muse Spark Open, on Baseten, compiles each rule three times; only what all three agree on ships.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
It reads fixed site cameras, the kind every warehouse already has.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Meta's SAM 3.1 tracks the truck, and finds the people from text alone. Here, a man walks into the truck zone with no hi-vis.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Four floor points per camera, marked once, give it a measured floor, so scripts measure speed and distance, as bands.

9
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:47,000
A script reads the colour on his torso: breach, with the frames and the number that decided it. Two people ahead of the truck, both vested: clear.

10
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Here the frame cuts the truck off, so nobody can see where it meets the floor. It won't guess: unable, naming the one fact it couldn't measure.

11
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Then real footage. A camera near the floor made it see a touch that never happened, so now under one and a half metres it won't rule on the floor.

12
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Nobody watches a whole shift. My demo shift: forty-eight windows, sixteen breaches to review, eighty-four clear, a hundred and forty unable — a hundred and ten of them, twenty-two windows times five rules, because the camera moved or was never marked.

13
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Back to racing. The steward names the moment; scripts cut the frames, the steward rules from them. Meta's models named the right offender four times in eight.

14
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
[On screen: the live site's "Check nobody changed it" replay, ending "Same ruling. Nobody edited it."]

15
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:47,000
On twenty-six graded clips it found twelve of fourteen breach clips, and no false breach on the ten compliant ones. Every ruling rebuilds offline to the same bytes.

16
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:57,000
A rules pack is just written rules — the same judge can be taught a crane site, a press, a crossing; those packs are written the same way this one was. Scripts decide, Meta's models add words, a person makes the call. That's faultline.
